From: OpenWrt
Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 05:40:00 +0200
Subject: clk: qcom: gcc-ipq6018: keep nss_crypto_pll enabled

nss_crypto_clk_src can only reach its 300 MHz operating point through
P_NSS_CRYPTO_PLL:

	static const struct freq_tbl ftbl_nss_crypto_clk_src[] = {
		F(24000000,  P_XO,             1, 0, 0),
		F(300000000, P_NSS_CRYPTO_PLL, 2, 0, 0),
		{ }
	};

The probe function configures nss_crypto_pll_main via
clk_alpha_pll_configure(), but configuring a PLL does not enable it.
Nothing else does either: of_clk_set_defaults() — which applies the
assigned-clock-rates of the PPE node — only calls clk_set_rate() and
never clk_prepare_enable(). The PLL therefore stays off, the RCG cannot
switch to a source that is not running, and its update bit never clears:

	nss_crypto_clk_src: rcg didn't update its configuration
	WARNING: CPU: 2 PID: 1 at drivers/clk/qcom/clk-rcg2.c:133
		update_config+0xac/0xc0
	...
	 clk_rcg2_set_rate_and_parent+0x2c/0x44
	 of_clk_set_defaults+0x234/0x4c0
	 qca_ppe_driver_init+0x1c/0x28

On the QSDK boot chain the PLL is already running when Linux starts, so
the problem does not show there. Booting the same SoC from a bootloader
that does not pre-enable it (for example MikroTik RouterBOOT) exposes it
on every boot.

Mark the PLL critical so the clock framework enables it during
registration, using the driver's own clk_alpha_pll_ops.enable() and thus
the proper lock sequence. This keeps the NSS crypto clock usable instead
of merely silencing the warning by dropping the rate assignment.

The same approach is already used for gcc_xo_clk_src.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt
---
 drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-ipq6018.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-ipq6018.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-ipq6018.c
@@ -254,6 +254,7 @@ static struct clk_alpha_pll nss_crypto_p
 				.fw_name = "xo",
 			},
 			.num_parents = 1,
+			.flags = CLK_IS_CRITICAL,
 			.ops = &clk_alpha_pll_ops,
 		},
 	},
